From 22f7b28157e214b272a59abfdd5a6fc255569f61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Satish Babariya <16605023+satishbabariya@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:27:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read a ClientHello split across records before judging its
 name

PeekServerName read exactly one TLS record. A ClientHello may legally be
split across several, and servers reassemble it: crypto/tls reads
records until the handshake message is whole. When the first record
held only part of the hello, the parser returned ErrIncomplete with no
name, and inspectAndForward treated every nameless error alike and
spliced the connection upstream. On a shared CDN address that let a
refused name through -- gist.github.com next to *.github.com -- for any
client that fragments its hello, which is the case the SNI check exists
for.

PeekServerName now reads handshake records until the ClientHello is
complete, bounded at 16 KiB for the message and 32 KiB for the records
carrying it, inside the existing 15 s deadline. Only the first record
decides whether the stream is TLS; once it is, anything else before the
hello is whole is ErrMalformed rather than "not TLS". The forwarder
splices only ErrNotTLS and a complete hello with no SNI, as before;
every other outcome is refused and audited as sni-unreadable.

The forwarder tests put a real crypto/tls client and server on either
side of inspectAndForward and re-frame the client's hello into two
records. Before this change the server completed a handshake for the
denied name; now it never sees the hello, while the same split for an
allowed name still completes.
